  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a broad concept for solar-driven interfacial evaporation as a multipurpose water technology linking water, energy, food, resource recovery, and wastewater treatment in coastal or island settings. At the center is a network of pipes connecting homes, farms, treatment units, industrial facilities, and nearshore devices, suggesting that evaporation-based systems can be integrated across a community.\n\nThe main message is that the same evaporation–condensation process can produce clean drinking water, support desalination for agriculture, and enable “water–electricity cogeneration.” Solar panels on the water and wind-powered farmland emphasize coupling freshwater production with renewable energy. Floating solar stills near the shore appear to represent decentralized freshwater generation.\n\nThe figure also highlights extraction of valuable products during evaporation, including hydrogen and critical metals such as lithium, implying that concentrated brines can be used as a resource rather than waste. On the opposite side, wastewater basins and circular treatment units show that interfacial solar evaporation can treat different kinds of wastewater.\n\nOverall, the image portrays evaporation not as a single desalination step, but as a platform technology for sustainable coastal infrastructure and circular resource use.",
